Paragraph 1

Responsibility for the Protection of Women's Rights Article 11 The responsibility of the family in efforts to protect women includes: a. protecting women from conflict and domestic violence that degrades the dignity of women;
b. providing equal opportunities for women to develop themselves in the fields of:
1. education;
2. social;
3. economy; and
4. politics and culture.  c. preventing violations of women's rights in the household.

Paragraph 2 Responsibility for the Protection of Children's Rights Article 12 (1) Parents in efforts to protect Children are responsible to: a. care for, maintain, educate, and protect Children; b. develop Children according to their abilities, talents, and interests;
c. provide formal and non-formal education;
d. guide Children to behave in accordance with religion, ethics and morals as well as customs; and e. prevent marriage at the age of Children.
2. In the event that parents do not exist, or their whereabouts are unknown, or due to a reason, cannot carry out their obligations and responsibilities, then the obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1) may be transferred to the family.

Article 7

1. The Regency Government in efforts to protect Women is obliged and responsible to: a. guarantee the Rights of Women without discrimination in accordance with the provisions of Laws and Regulations;
b. establish and implement policies, programs and activities for the protection of Women;
c. cooperate with the community and/or Community Organizations in the implementation of Women's Protection in accordance with the provisions of Laws and Regulations;
d. carry out rehabilitation and empowerment of Women who are victims of violence;
e. provide special protection to Women victims of violence and trafficking in persons; and f. take preventive action in order to prevent violations of the rights of Women.
2. The responsibility of the Regency Government in efforts to protect the Rights of Women as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be implemented by DPPPA.

Article 8 (1) The Regency Government in efforts to protect Children is obliged and responsible to:
a. respect the fulfillment of the rights of Children without distinguishing ethnicity, religion, race, class, gender, ethnicity, culture and language, legal status, birth order, and physical and/or mental condition;
b. provide support for facilities, infrastructure, and availability of human resources in the implementation of Child Protection;
c. guarantee the protection, care, and welfare of Children by taking into account the rights and obligations of Parents, Guardians, or other persons who are legally responsible for the Child;
d. guarantee Children to exercise their right to express opinions in accordance with the age and level of intelligence of the Child;
e. establish and implement policies and programs/activities for the protection of the rights of Children;
f. cooperate with the community and/or Community Organizations in the implementation of Child protection in accordance with the provisions of Laws and Regulations;
g. carry out rehabilitation of Children who are victims of violence;
h. provide special protection to Children:
1. in emergency situations victims of disasters and conflicts;
2. in conflict with the law;
3. victims of violence, neglect and trafficking in persons; 4. from minority and isolated groups;
5. who are exploited economically and/or sexually/victims of pornography;
6. who are victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic abuse; and
7. identified with HIV/AIDS;
i. organize inclusive education for Children with special needs;
j. take preventive action in order to prevent violations against Children; and k. implement and support National policies in the implementation of Child Protection in the Regency carried out through efforts to realize Child-Friendly Regency, Child-Friendly Sub-district and Child-Friendly Village;
2. The responsibility of the Regency Government in efforts to protect Children as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be implemented by DPPPA.

Article 9 (1) The Regency Government in carrying out its responsibilities, establishes programs and activities for the protection of Women and Children, with a Regional Action Plan as the basis for Regional Apparatus in implementing the protection of Women and Children.

2. Further provisions regarding the Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be regulated by Regent Regulation.

Part Three

Social Rehabilitation Services for Women and Children Victims of Violence Article 45 (1) Social rehabilitation services for Women and Children victims of violence are aimed at restoring and developing the abilities of victims who experience social dysfunction to be able to carry out their social functions in society properly.

2. Social rehabilitation services as referred to in paragraph (1) include: a. counseling services;
b. provision of safe houses;
c. spiritual guidance.
3. The implementation of services as referred to in paragraph (2) is carried out by: a. Regional Apparatus having duties and functions in the social field; b. DPPPA;
c. P2TP2A;
d. Women Crisis Centre (WCC);
e. social community organizations; and/or f. religious institutions.

Article 46 (1) Counseling services as referred to in Article 45 paragraph (2) letter a aim to:
a. help victims recognize their problems and find effective ways to overcome them themselves;
b. empower victims to determine their future;
c. provide moral support for victims in facing the process they are undergoing; and d. make victims feel accepted in the community environment. 25 (2) Counseling services are carried out by gender-responsive social rehabilitation officers, consisting of: a. counselors;
b. psychologists; and c. psychiatrists.
3. Counseling services are carried out in a place that guarantees a sense of security, comfort and confidentiality of information from victims.
4. Stages of counseling services include: a. initial counseling;
b. follow-up counseling;
c. case conference; and d. home visit.

Article 47 (1) The Regency Government may provide a safe house for Women and Children victims of violence.

2. The safe house as referred to in paragraph (1) aims to protect Women and Children victims of violence from intimidation by perpetrators or other parties.
3. The location of the safe house as referred to in paragraph (1) must be kept confidential.
4. Criteria for Women and Children victims of violence who require a safe house include: a. their life is threatened;
b. receiving rejection from family or community;
c. requiring intensive services but their residence is relatively far; and/or d. abandoned if not placed in a safe house.
5. The implementation of safe house services is carried out by social rehabilitation officers who have gender sensitivity or gender awareness, consisting of: a. social workers/volunteers;
b. security officers; and c. community leaders.
6. Further provisions regarding the implementation of safe house services are regulated by Regent Regulation.

Article 48 (1) Every Woman and Child victim of violence is provided with spiritual guidance aimed at psychological recovery.

2. Spiritual guidance as referred to in paragraph (1) is implemented based on the principles of: a. not imposing any religion or belief; and b. leaving the final decision in the hands of the victim (self determination). (3) Spiritual guidance as referred to in paragraph (2) is carried out by spiritual guidance officers.
4. Spiritual guidance officers as referred to in paragraph (3) consist of: a. consultants of the Marriage Advisory, Development and Preservation Agency (BP4);
b. Officers of the Office of Religious Affairs (KUA);
c. Ustad or Ulama;
d. Pastor;
e. Minister; and •f. other religious leaders who have gender sensitivity or gender awareness.

CHAPTER VI

HANDLING

Part One

Handling of Women and Children Victims of Violence Article 40 The implementation of handling Women and Children victims of violence includes: a. handling complaints of victims of violence;
b. health services;
c. social rehabilitation;
d. law enforcement and legal assistance; and e. repatriation and social reintegration.

Article 41 (1) The implementation of handling Women and Children victims of violence as referred to in Article 40 shall be carried out through integrated services.

2. Integrated services for Women and Children victims of violence as referred to in paragraph (1) use the principles of: a. gender responsive;
b. non-discrimination;
c. equal and respectful relationships;
d. maintaining confidentiality;
e. providing a sense of security and comfort;
f. respecting individual differences;
g. non-judgmental;
h. respecting the choices and decisions of victims;
i. sensitive to the background and condition of victims and the use of language that is appropriate and understood by victims;
j. fast and simple;
k. empathy;

I. fulfillment of the rights of Women and Children; and
m. free of charge.

Article 42 Services for handling complaints of victims of violence against Women and Children shall be carried out by:
a. hospitals;
b. community health centers;
c. P2TP2A;
d. Women and Children Service Unit (UPPA);
e. Trauma Center Shelter (RPTC);
f. Children's Social Shelter (RPSA);
g. Women Crisis Centre (WCC) h. Advisory Board for Marriage Guidance and Preservation (BP4); i. Police;
j. Prosecutor's Office;
k. Court;

I. Legal Aid Institute (LBH); and

m. Child Protection Institute (LPA).

Article 43 (1) Complaints of acts of violence against Women and Children as referred to in Article 42 may be made by reporters consisting of:

a. Victims;

b. Family;
c. Community; and I or

d. community organizations in the field of protection of Women and Children.

2. Reporters as referred to in paragraph (1) may make complaints: a. directly;
b. by telephone; and c. by letter.
3. In addition to handling complaints submitted by reporters, officers at service institutions as referred to in Article 42 are authorized to handle victims of violence against Women and Children obtained through other information.
4. Further provisions regarding the procedures for handling complaint services as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2) shall be regulated by Regent Regulation

Part Two

Health Services for Women and Children Victims of Violence I Article 44 (1) Health services for Women and Children victims of violence include aspects of:

a. Promotive;

b. Preventive;

c. Curative;

d. rehabilitative.

2. Health services for Women and Children victims of violence shall be carried out a. Comprehensively;
b. accessible 24 (twenty-four) hours;
c. with quality; and d. in accordance with health service standards.
3. Health services specifically for Children victims of violence, must I prioritize the best interests of the Child.
4. Women and Children victims of violence are entitled to health services, I including a. medical services;
b. medicolegal services; and c. psychosocial counseling.
5. Further provisions regarding the procedures for implementing health services as referred to in paragraph (4) shall be regulated by Regent Regulation

CHAPTER II

RIGHTS OF WOMEN AND RIGHTS OF CHILDREN

Part One

Rights of Women Article 5 Every Woman has the right to:
a. obtain protection from acts of discrimination;
b. obtain protection from economic exploitation and sexual exploitation; c. obtain protection from physical, psychological, sexual violence, and neglect;
d. obtain decent work according to her ability; e. obtain work leave while receiving wages/salary in accordance with statutory regulations;
f. obtain education and teaching in all types, levels and pathways of education;
g. obtain special protection in the performance of work or profession against matters that may threaten safety and/or health in connection with the reproductive function of Women; andh. ownership and management of joint assets in marriage in accordance with the provisions of Laws and Regulations.

CHAPTER IV

PREVENTION

Part One

Regency Government Article 13 (1) Efforts made by the Regency Government in order to prevent violations of the rights of Women include:
a. organizing socialization regarding the prevention of violence against Women by optimizing the role of customary, cultural, religious institutions and community organizations;
b. providing guidelines on handling violence against Women;
c. involving community participation in recovery and reintegration; d. developing databases and integrated information systems on violence against Women;
e. developing family empowerment programs;
f. strengthening the function of community organizations engaged in Women's empowerment; and g. providing public facilities that are comfortable and safe for Women.
2. Efforts made by the Regency Government in order to prevent violations of the rights of Children include: a. prevention of violence against Children by optimizing the role of customary, cultural, religious institutions and community organizations; b. developing child-friendly schools;
c. encouraging increased child participation through the establishment of Children's Forums;
d. providing guidelines on handling violence against Children;
e. involving community participation in recovery and reintegration services for child victims of violence;
f. developing databases and integrated information systems on violence against Children;
g. strengthening the function of community organizations engaged in Child protection; and h. encouraging the establishment of Child-Friendly Regency.

Paragraph 1

Health Article 23 (1) The Regency Government's efforts to provide protection for Children in the health sector include: a. free health services for Children from poor families;
b. implementation of optimal health for Children so that they obtain a good degree of health from the womb;
c. ensuring that infants receive exclusive breastfeeding from their mothers properly and naturally;
d. ensuring Children receive adequate nutrition;
e. ensuring newborn Children undergo screening; and f. ensuring the provision of monitoring of growth and development of toddlers and pre-school age children.
2. The Regency Government, family, and parents protect children from attempts to transplant their body organs to other parties.

Paragraph 2 Education Article 24 Children in formal and non-formal educational institutions must be protected from acts of violence.

,I Article 25 Every Child aged 7 (seven) to 18 (eighteen) years who has not completed education, exceeding 3 (three) years beyond the age limit of formal education may pursue education through non-formal education units:
a. Package A equivalency program equivalent to Elementary School and Madrasah Ibtidaiyah;
b. Package B equivalency program equivalent to Junior High School and Madrasah Tsanawiyah; and c. Package C equivalency program equivalent to Senior High School, Madrasah Aliyah and Vocational High School.

Article 26 (1) Children with special needs and/or who have potential intelligence and/or special talents are given equal opportunities and facilities to obtain regular education or inclusive education.

2. Inclusive education as referred to in paragraph (1) is organized by the Regency Government and the community.

Article 27 Every organizer of formal education is prohibited from expelling Children without guaranteeing the continuity of the Child's education.

Article 28 (1) The Regency Government provides scholarships and/or educational assistance for:
a. children from poor families;
b. neglected children; and c. children residing in remote areas.
2. The implementation of scholarship programs and/or educational assistance as referred to in paragraph (1) is supported by the participation of the community and community organizations.
3. Further provisions regarding the provision of scholarships and or educational assistance as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be regulated by Regent Regulation.

Article 29 Children in conflict with the law, Children victims of violence and sexual abuse and Children victims of HIV/AIDS transmission are protected in their rights to obtain education.

Part Two

Rights of Children Article 6 (1)Every Child has the right to:
a. live, grow, develop, and participate reasonably in accordance with human dignity and dignity, and receive protection from violence and discrimination;
b. obtain a name as personal identity and citizenship status; c. worship according to their religion, think, and express themselves in accordance with the level of intelligence and age under the guidance of Parents or Guardians; d. obtain education and teaching in the context of personal development and level of intelligence in accordance with interests and talents and receive protection in educational units from sexual crimes and Violence committed by educators, education personnel, fellow students, and/or other parties;
e. obtain protection from being the target of persecution, torture, or imposition of inhuman punishment;
f. obtain freedom in accordance with the law and arrest, detention, or criminal imprisonment of Children shall only be carried out if in accordance with applicable law and can only be carried out as a last resort;
g. be cared for by their own Parents, unless there are reasons and/or valid legal rules showing that the separation is in the best interests of the Child and is a last consideration;
h. obtain protection from abuse in political activities, involvement in armed conflicts, involvement in social unrest, involvement in events containing elements of Violence, involvement in warfare and, sexual crimes;
i. participate, express opinions and think in accordance with conscience and religion;

j. receive oral or written information in accordance with the age stage and development of the Child;
k. associate and assemble; and
l. rest, play, recreate, create, and engage in cultural arts, obtain play facilities that meet health and safety requirements and are adapted to the age of the Child, the ability level of the Child, and the environment so as not to hinder and disturb. (2)Every child who is deprived of liberty has the right to:

a.be treated humanely;

b.placement separated from adults;

c.obtain legal assistance or other assistance effectively at every stage of applicable legal remedies; and

d.defend themselves and obtain justice before an objective and impartial Juvenile Court in a closed session to the public. (3)Every child who is a victim or perpetrator of sexual violence or who is in conflict with the law has the right to confidentiality.

4. Every Child with Disabilities in addition to obtaining rights as referred to in paragraph (1) is also entitled to obtain:

a.special education/special education;

b.rehabilitation;
c. social assistance; and

d.maintenance of social welfare standards.

5. Every Child who has excellence has the right to receive special education.

CHAPTER III

Part Three

Parents and/or Family Article 15 (1) Efforts made by the family in order to prevent violations of the rights of Women include:
a. protection of women from various violations of rights within the household; and b. encouraging the formation of harmonious, educative and democratic families;
2. Efforts made by parents in order to prevent violations of the rights of Children include: a. providing good examples to children in accordance with religious and customary teachings;
b. supervision of children in their activities inside or outside the home;
c. providing opportunities for children to engage in dialogue and express opinions democratically; and d. protection of children from various acts of violation of the rights of Children within the household.

Part Four

Law Enforcement and Legal Aid Services I, Article 49 (1) Law enforcement and legal aid services are actions of law enforcement officers provided to Women and Children victims of violence and/or witnesses.

2. Law enforcement and legal aid services as referred to in paragraph (1) are carried out by prioritizing the best interests of victims.

Article 50 (1) Law enforcement and legal aid services as referred to in article 49 are aimed at providing assistance and protection to Women and Children victims of violence and witnesses with a gender-responsive approach so that victims obtain justice and legal certainty.

2. Types of legal aid services as referred to in paragraph (1) include: a. legal aid in the reporting process and preparation of BAP; b. legal aid in the prosecution process at the prosecutor's office;
c. legal aid in the examination process at court; d. legal aid in the court hearing decision process until there is a final legal decision; and e. legal aid in the mediation process.
3. The Regency Government provides legal aid to Children and Women victims of violence free of charge.
4. In providing legal aid as referred to in paragraph (3) the Regency Government may cooperate with: a. Legal Aid Institute (LBH);
b. Women Crisis Center (WCC);
c. Advocates; and/or d. other legal assistants who are gender sensitive.

Article 30

The Regency Government and the community shall organize Early Childhood Education (PAUD).

Paragraph 3 Employment Article 31 Employers are prohibited from employing Children.

Article 32 (1) The provisions as referred to in Article 31 may be exempted for Children aged between 13 (thirteen) years to 15 (fifteen) years, to perform light work as long as it does not interfere with physical, mental, and social development and health.

2. Employers who employ Children in light work as referred to in paragraph (1) must fulfill requirements including: a. written permission from parents or guardian;
b. work agreement between employer and parents or guardian; c. maximum working time of 3 (three) hours;
d. performed during the day and does not interfere with school time; e. occupational safety and health;
f. clear employment relationship; and g. receive wages in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.
3. The provisions as referred to in paragraph (2) letter a, letter b, letter f, and letter g are exempted for Children who work in their family business.

Article 33 In the event that a company employs Children as referred to in Article 32, then the workplace of Children must be separated from the workplace of adult workers.

Article 34 (1) Children may perform work in a workplace that is part of an educational or training curriculum authorized by the competent official.

2. Children as referred to in paragraph (1) must be at least 14 I (fourteen) years old.

I\ Article 35 (1) Every person is prohibited from employing and involving Children in the worst forms of work.

2. The worst forms of work as referred to in paragraph (1) include: a. all work in the form of slavery or similar practices; b. all work that exploits, provides, or offers Children for . 1. prostitution;
2. pornography production;
3. pornographic performances;
4. gambling;
5. production and trade of alcoholic beverages;
6. narcotics;
7. psychotropic substances; and
8. other addictive substances; and l or c. all work that endangers the health, safety, or morals of Children.

Paragraph 4 Social Article 36 (1) The Regency Government is obliged to provide care for neglected Children.

2. Care as referred to in paragraph (1) may be carried out by Community Organizations in the social field.
3. In the event that a Child is neglected because for some reason the parents neglect their obligations, then the Family or Regency Government or Community Organization as referred to in paragraph (2) may submit an application to the court to determine the Child as a neglected Child.
4. The court determination as referred to in paragraph (3) simultaneously determines the shelter and care for the neglected Child concerned.

Article 37 (1) The Regency Government shall organize skills training for street Children and neglected Children.

2. Skills training as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be organized by Regional Apparatus whose duties and functions are in the social and employment fields and may cooperate with Community Organizations.

Article 38 (1) The Regency Government shall provide shelters to foster street Children and social institutions for neglected Children.

2. Shelters as referred to in paragraph (1) may be organized in cooperation with Community Organizations.
3. Further provisions regarding shelters as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be regulated by Regent Regulation Article 39 The implementation of protection for Women and Children shall be carried out by Regional Apparatus whose duties and functions are related to the protection of Women and Children.

Part Two

Definitions, Meanings and Terminology Article 2 (1) Implementation of Protection of Women is based on the principles of:
a. best interests of Women;
b. gender justice and gender equality;
c. local wisdom; andd. legal certainty.
2. Implementation of Protection of Children is based on the principles of: a. non-discrimination;
b. best interests of the Child;
c. right to life, survival, and development; d. respect for the views of the Child; ande. legal certainty.

Article 3 The regulation of Protection of Women and Children aims to:
a. serve as a guideline for the Regency Government in planning, policies and strategies for Protection of Women and Children;
b. protect Women and Children from actions, decisions and policies that violate the rights of Women and Children;
c. prevent violations of the Rights of Women and Children; d. increase empowerment of Women and Children; e. increase the role and value of local wisdom in the Protection of Women and Children; andf. increase the role of Regional Apparatus and non-Government entities in the Regency in efforts to Protect Women and Children.

Article 4 The scope of regulation of Protection of Women and Children includes: a. prevention;
b. implementation of Protection of the Rights of Women and Children; c. handling;
d. empowerment; ande. guidance and supervision.

REGENT OF MUSI BANYUASIN

PROVINCE OF SOUTH SUMATRA

REGIONAL REGULATION OF MUSI BANYUASIN REGENCY NUMBER II OF 2018

CONCERNING

PROTECTION OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN

BY THE GRACE OF GOD ALMIGHTY

REGENT OF MUSI BANYUASIN,
a. that the government has an obligation to respect, protect and fulfill the Human Rights of every citizen including the Rights of Women and Children;
b. that Women and Children are vulnerable groups who tend to experience violence and discrimination and therefore need to receive optimal protection;
c. that the fulfillment of the constitutional rights of Women and Children as well as the improvement of the quality of life of Women and Children, is one of the mandatory affairs that is the duty, authority and responsibility of the Regency Government;
d. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, letter b and letter c, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation on the Protection of Women and Children;
1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;
2. Law Number 28 of 1959 concerning the Establishment of Level II Regions and Municipalities in South Sumatra (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1959 Number 73, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 1821);

Decides: I

3. Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2002 Number 109, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4235), as amended by Law Number 35 of 2014 concerning Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 297, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5606);
4. Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 244, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5587), as amended several times, most recently by Law Number 9 of 2015 concerning the Second Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2015 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5679);

With Mutual Approval

