Part Four

Law Enforcement and Legal Aid Services I, Article 49 (1) Law enforcement and legal aid services are actions of law enforcement officers provided to Women and Children victims of violence and/or witnesses.

2. Law enforcement and legal aid services as referred to in paragraph (1) are carried out by prioritizing the best interests of victims.

Article 50 (1) Law enforcement and legal aid services as referred to in article 49 are aimed at providing assistance and protection to Women and Children victims of violence and witnesses with a gender-responsive approach so that victims obtain justice and legal certainty.

2. Types of legal aid services as referred to in paragraph (1) include: a. legal aid in the reporting process and preparation of BAP; b. legal aid in the prosecution process at the prosecutor's office;
c. legal aid in the examination process at court; d. legal aid in the court hearing decision process until there is a final legal decision; and e. legal aid in the mediation process.
3. The Regency Government provides legal aid to Children and Women victims of violence free of charge.
4. In providing legal aid as referred to in paragraph (3) the Regency Government may cooperate with: a. Legal Aid Institute (LBH);
b. Women Crisis Center (WCC);
c. Advocates; and/or d. other legal assistants who are gender sensitive.

Part Two

Rights of Children Article 6 (1)Every Child has the right to:
a. live, grow, develop, and participate reasonably in accordance with human dignity and dignity, and receive protection from violence and discrimination;
b. obtain a name as personal identity and citizenship status; c. worship according to their religion, think, and express themselves in accordance with the level of intelligence and age under the guidance of Parents or Guardians; d. obtain education and teaching in the context of personal development and level of intelligence in accordance with interests and talents and receive protection in educational units from sexual crimes and Violence committed by educators, education personnel, fellow students, and/or other parties;
e. obtain protection from being the target of persecution, torture, or imposition of inhuman punishment;
f. obtain freedom in accordance with the law and arrest, detention, or criminal imprisonment of Children shall only be carried out if in accordance with applicable law and can only be carried out as a last resort;
g. be cared for by their own Parents, unless there are reasons and/or valid legal rules showing that the separation is in the best interests of the Child and is a last consideration;
h. obtain protection from abuse in political activities, involvement in armed conflicts, involvement in social unrest, involvement in events containing elements of Violence, involvement in warfare and, sexual crimes;
i. participate, express opinions and think in accordance with conscience and religion;

j. receive oral or written information in accordance with the age stage and development of the Child;
k. associate and assemble; and
l. rest, play, recreate, create, and engage in cultural arts, obtain play facilities that meet health and safety requirements and are adapted to the age of the Child, the ability level of the Child, and the environment so as not to hinder and disturb. (2)Every child who is deprived of liberty has the right to:

a.be treated humanely;

b.placement separated from adults;

c.obtain legal assistance or other assistance effectively at every stage of applicable legal remedies; and

d.defend themselves and obtain justice before an objective and impartial Juvenile Court in a closed session to the public. (3)Every child who is a victim or perpetrator of sexual violence or who is in conflict with the law has the right to confidentiality.

4. Every Child with Disabilities in addition to obtaining rights as referred to in paragraph (1) is also entitled to obtain:

a.special education/special education;

b.rehabilitation;
c. social assistance; and

d.maintenance of social welfare standards.

5. Every Child who has excellence has the right to receive special education.

CHAPTER III

Paragraph 1

Health Article 23 (1) The Regency Government's efforts to provide protection for Children in the health sector include: a. free health services for Children from poor families;
b. implementation of optimal health for Children so that they obtain a good degree of health from the womb;
c. ensuring that infants receive exclusive breastfeeding from their mothers properly and naturally;
d. ensuring Children receive adequate nutrition;
e. ensuring newborn Children undergo screening; and f. ensuring the provision of monitoring of growth and development of toddlers and pre-school age children.
2. The Regency Government, family, and parents protect children from attempts to transplant their body organs to other parties.

Paragraph 2 Education Article 24 Children in formal and non-formal educational institutions must be protected from acts of violence.

,I Article 25 Every Child aged 7 (seven) to 18 (eighteen) years who has not completed education, exceeding 3 (three) years beyond the age limit of formal education may pursue education through non-formal education units:
a. Package A equivalency program equivalent to Elementary School and Madrasah Ibtidaiyah;
b. Package B equivalency program equivalent to Junior High School and Madrasah Tsanawiyah; and c. Package C equivalency program equivalent to Senior High School, Madrasah Aliyah and Vocational High School.

Article 26 (1) Children with special needs and/or who have potential intelligence and/or special talents are given equal opportunities and facilities to obtain regular education or inclusive education.

2. Inclusive education as referred to in paragraph (1) is organized by the Regency Government and the community.

Article 27 Every organizer of formal education is prohibited from expelling Children without guaranteeing the continuity of the Child's education.

Article 28 (1) The Regency Government provides scholarships and/or educational assistance for:
a. children from poor families;
b. neglected children; and c. children residing in remote areas.
2. The implementation of scholarship programs and/or educational assistance as referred to in paragraph (1) is supported by the participation of the community and community organizations.
3. Further provisions regarding the provision of scholarships and or educational assistance as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be regulated by Regent Regulation.

Article 29 Children in conflict with the law, Children victims of violence and sexual abuse and Children victims of HIV/AIDS transmission are protected in their rights to obtain education.

