4. Further provisions regarding the procedures for handling complaint services as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2) shall be regulated by Regent Regulation

Part Three

Social Rehabilitation Services for Women and Children Victims of Violence Article 45 (1) Social rehabilitation services for Women and Children victims of violence are aimed at restoring and developing the abilities of victims who experience social dysfunction to be able to carry out their social functions in society properly.

2. Social rehabilitation services as referred to in paragraph (1) include: a. counseling services;
b. provision of safe houses;
c. spiritual guidance.
3. The implementation of services as referred to in paragraph (2) is carried out by: a. Regional Apparatus having duties and functions in the social field; b. DPPPA;
c. P2TP2A;
d. Women Crisis Centre (WCC);
e. social community organizations; and/or f. religious institutions.

Article 46 (1) Counseling services as referred to in Article 45 paragraph (2) letter a aim to:
a. help victims recognize their problems and find effective ways to overcome them themselves;
b. empower victims to determine their future;
c. provide moral support for victims in facing the process they are undergoing; and d. make victims feel accepted in the community environment. 25 (2) Counseling services are carried out by gender-responsive social rehabilitation officers, consisting of: a. counselors;
b. psychologists; and c. psychiatrists.
3. Counseling services are carried out in a place that guarantees a sense of security, comfort and confidentiality of information from victims.
4. Stages of counseling services include: a. initial counseling;
b. follow-up counseling;
c. case conference; and d. home visit.

Article 47 (1) The Regency Government may provide a safe house for Women and Children victims of violence.

2. The safe house as referred to in paragraph (1) aims to protect Women and Children victims of violence from intimidation by perpetrators or other parties.
3. The location of the safe house as referred to in paragraph (1) must be kept confidential.
4. Criteria for Women and Children victims of violence who require a safe house include: a. their life is threatened;
b. receiving rejection from family or community;
c. requiring intensive services but their residence is relatively far; and/or d. abandoned if not placed in a safe house.
5. The implementation of safe house services is carried out by social rehabilitation officers who have gender sensitivity or gender awareness, consisting of: a. social workers/volunteers;
b. security officers; and c. community leaders.
6. Further provisions regarding the implementation of safe house services are regulated by Regent Regulation.

Article 48 (1) Every Woman and Child victim of violence is provided with spiritual guidance aimed at psychological recovery.

2. Spiritual guidance as referred to in paragraph (1) is implemented based on the principles of: a. not imposing any religion or belief; and b. leaving the final decision in the hands of the victim (self determination). (3) Spiritual guidance as referred to in paragraph (2) is carried out by spiritual guidance officers.
4. Spiritual guidance officers as referred to in paragraph (3) consist of: a. consultants of the Marriage Advisory, Development and Preservation Agency (BP4);
b. Officers of the Office of Religious Affairs (KUA);
c. Ustad or Ulama;
d. Pastor;
e. Minister; and •f. other religious leaders who have gender sensitivity or gender awareness.

