PREVENTION

Part One

Regency Government Article 13 (1) Efforts made by the Regency Government in order to prevent violations of the rights of Women include:
a. organizing socialization regarding the prevention of violence against Women by optimizing the role of customary, cultural, religious institutions and community organizations;
b. providing guidelines on handling violence against Women;
c. involving community participation in recovery and reintegration; d. developing databases and integrated information systems on violence against Women;
e. developing family empowerment programs;
f. strengthening the function of community organizations engaged in Women's empowerment; and g. providing public facilities that are comfortable and safe for Women.
2. Efforts made by the Regency Government in order to prevent violations of the rights of Children include: a. prevention of violence against Children by optimizing the role of customary, cultural, religious institutions and community organizations; b. developing child-friendly schools;
c. encouraging increased child participation through the establishment of Children's Forums;
d. providing guidelines on handling violence against Children;
e. involving community participation in recovery and reintegration services for child victims of violence;
f. developing databases and integrated information systems on violence against Children;
g. strengthening the function of community organizations engaged in Child protection; and h. encouraging the establishment of Child-Friendly Regency.

Article 7

1. The Regency Government in efforts to protect Women is obliged and responsible to: a. guarantee the Rights of Women without discrimination in accordance with the provisions of Laws and Regulations;
b. establish and implement policies, programs and activities for the protection of Women;
c. cooperate with the community and/or Community Organizations in the implementation of Women's Protection in accordance with the provisions of Laws and Regulations;
d. carry out rehabilitation and empowerment of Women who are victims of violence;
e. provide special protection to Women victims of violence and trafficking in persons; and f. take preventive action in order to prevent violations of the rights of Women.
2. The responsibility of the Regency Government in efforts to protect the Rights of Women as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be implemented by DPPPA.

Article 8 (1) The Regency Government in efforts to protect Children is obliged and responsible to:
a. respect the fulfillment of the rights of Children without distinguishing ethnicity, religion, race, class, gender, ethnicity, culture and language, legal status, birth order, and physical and/or mental condition;
b. provide support for facilities, infrastructure, and availability of human resources in the implementation of Child Protection;
c. guarantee the protection, care, and welfare of Children by taking into account the rights and obligations of Parents, Guardians, or other persons who are legally responsible for the Child;
d. guarantee Children to exercise their right to express opinions in accordance with the age and level of intelligence of the Child;
e. establish and implement policies and programs/activities for the protection of the rights of Children;
f. cooperate with the community and/or Community Organizations in the implementation of Child protection in accordance with the provisions of Laws and Regulations;
g. carry out rehabilitation of Children who are victims of violence;
h. provide special protection to Children:
1. in emergency situations victims of disasters and conflicts;
2. in conflict with the law;
3. victims of violence, neglect and trafficking in persons; 4. from minority and isolated groups;
5. who are exploited economically and/or sexually/victims of pornography;
6. who are victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic abuse; and
7. identified with HIV/AIDS;
i. organize inclusive education for Children with special needs;
j. take preventive action in order to prevent violations against Children; and k. implement and support National policies in the implementation of Child Protection in the Regency carried out through efforts to realize Child-Friendly Regency, Child-Friendly Sub-district and Child-Friendly Village;
2. The responsibility of the Regency Government in efforts to protect Children as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be implemented by DPPPA.

Article 9 (1) The Regency Government in carrying out its responsibilities, establishes programs and activities for the protection of Women and Children, with a Regional Action Plan as the basis for Regional Apparatus in implementing the protection of Women and Children.

2. Further provisions regarding the Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be regulated by Regent Regulation.

CHAPTER II

RIGHTS OF WOMEN AND RIGHTS OF CHILDREN

Part One

Rights of Women Article 5 Every Woman has the right to:
a. obtain protection from acts of discrimination;
b. obtain protection from economic exploitation and sexual exploitation; c. obtain protection from physical, psychological, sexual violence, and neglect;
d. obtain decent work according to her ability; e. obtain work leave while receiving wages/salary in accordance with statutory regulations;
f. obtain education and teaching in all types, levels and pathways of education;
g. obtain special protection in the performance of work or profession against matters that may threaten safety and/or health in connection with the reproductive function of Women; andh. ownership and management of joint assets in marriage in accordance with the provisions of Laws and Regulations.

Part Two

Rights of Children Article 6 (1)Every Child has the right to:
a. live, grow, develop, and participate reasonably in accordance with human dignity and dignity, and receive protection from violence and discrimination;
b. obtain a name as personal identity and citizenship status; c. worship according to their religion, think, and express themselves in accordance with the level of intelligence and age under the guidance of Parents or Guardians; d. obtain education and teaching in the context of personal development and level of intelligence in accordance with interests and talents and receive protection in educational units from sexual crimes and Violence committed by educators, education personnel, fellow students, and/or other parties;
e. obtain protection from being the target of persecution, torture, or imposition of inhuman punishment;
f. obtain freedom in accordance with the law and arrest, detention, or criminal imprisonment of Children shall only be carried out if in accordance with applicable law and can only be carried out as a last resort;
g. be cared for by their own Parents, unless there are reasons and/or valid legal rules showing that the separation is in the best interests of the Child and is a last consideration;
h. obtain protection from abuse in political activities, involvement in armed conflicts, involvement in social unrest, involvement in events containing elements of Violence, involvement in warfare and, sexual crimes;
i. participate, express opinions and think in accordance with conscience and religion;

j. receive oral or written information in accordance with the age stage and development of the Child;
k. associate and assemble; and
l. rest, play, recreate, create, and engage in cultural arts, obtain play facilities that meet health and safety requirements and are adapted to the age of the Child, the ability level of the Child, and the environment so as not to hinder and disturb. (2)Every child who is deprived of liberty has the right to:

a.be treated humanely;

b.placement separated from adults;

c.obtain legal assistance or other assistance effectively at every stage of applicable legal remedies; and

d.defend themselves and obtain justice before an objective and impartial Juvenile Court in a closed session to the public. (3)Every child who is a victim or perpetrator of sexual violence or who is in conflict with the law has the right to confidentiality.

4. Every Child with Disabilities in addition to obtaining rights as referred to in paragraph (1) is also entitled to obtain:

a.special education/special education;

b.rehabilitation;
c. social assistance; and

d.maintenance of social welfare standards.

5. Every Child who has excellence has the right to receive special education.

CHAPTER III

Paragraph 1

Responsibility for the Protection of Women's Rights Article 11 The responsibility of the family in efforts to protect women includes: a. protecting women from conflict and domestic violence that degrades the dignity of women;
b. providing equal opportunities for women to develop themselves in the fields of:
1. education;
2. social;
3. economy; and
4. politics and culture.  c. preventing violations of women's rights in the household.

Paragraph 2 Responsibility for the Protection of Children's Rights Article 12 (1) Parents in efforts to protect Children are responsible to: a. care for, maintain, educate, and protect Children; b. develop Children according to their abilities, talents, and interests;
c. provide formal and non-formal education;
d. guide Children to behave in accordance with religion, ethics and morals as well as customs; and e. prevent marriage at the age of Children.
2. In the event that parents do not exist, or their whereabouts are unknown, or due to a reason, cannot carry out their obligations and responsibilities, then the obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1) may be transferred to the family.

