4. Stages of counseling services include: a. initial counseling;
b. follow-up counseling;
c. case conference; and d. home visit.

Article 47 (1) The Regency Government may provide a safe house for Women and Children victims of violence.

2. The safe house as referred to in paragraph (1) aims to protect Women and Children victims of violence from intimidation by perpetrators or other parties.
3. The location of the safe house as referred to in paragraph (1) must be kept confidential.
4. Criteria for Women and Children victims of violence who require a safe house include: a. their life is threatened;
b. receiving rejection from family or community;
c. requiring intensive services but their residence is relatively far; and/or d. abandoned if not placed in a safe house.
5. The implementation of safe house services is carried out by social rehabilitation officers who have gender sensitivity or gender awareness, consisting of: a. social workers/volunteers;
b. security officers; and c. community leaders.
6. Further provisions regarding the implementation of safe house services are regulated by Regent Regulation.

Article 48 (1) Every Woman and Child victim of violence is provided with spiritual guidance aimed at psychological recovery.

2. Spiritual guidance as referred to in paragraph (1) is implemented based on the principles of: a. not imposing any religion or belief; and b. leaving the final decision in the hands of the victim (self determination). (3) Spiritual guidance as referred to in paragraph (2) is carried out by spiritual guidance officers.
4. Spiritual guidance officers as referred to in paragraph (3) consist of: a. consultants of the Marriage Advisory, Development and Preservation Agency (BP4);
b. Officers of the Office of Religious Affairs (KUA);
c. Ustad or Ulama;
d. Pastor;
e. Minister; and •f. other religious leaders who have gender sensitivity or gender awareness.

REGENT OF MUSI BANYUASIN

PROVINCE OF SOUTH SUMATRA

REGIONAL REGULATION OF MUSI BANYUASIN REGENCY NUMBER II OF 2018

CONCERNING

PROTECTION OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN

BY THE GRACE OF GOD ALMIGHTY

REGENT OF MUSI BANYUASIN,
a. that the government has an obligation to respect, protect and fulfill the Human Rights of every citizen including the Rights of Women and Children;
b. that Women and Children are vulnerable groups who tend to experience violence and discrimination and therefore need to receive optimal protection;
c. that the fulfillment of the constitutional rights of Women and Children as well as the improvement of the quality of life of Women and Children, is one of the mandatory affairs that is the duty, authority and responsibility of the Regency Government;
d. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, letter b and letter c, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation on the Protection of Women and Children;
1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;
2. Law Number 28 of 1959 concerning the Establishment of Level II Regions and Municipalities in South Sumatra (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1959 Number 73, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 1821);

Decides: I

3. Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2002 Number 109, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4235), as amended by Law Number 35 of 2014 concerning Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 297, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5606);
4. Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 244, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5587), as amended several times, most recently by Law Number 9 of 2015 concerning the Second Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2015 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5679);

With Mutual Approval

Paragraph 1

Responsibility for the Protection of Women's Rights Article 11 The responsibility of the family in efforts to protect women includes: a. protecting women from conflict and domestic violence that degrades the dignity of women;
b. providing equal opportunities for women to develop themselves in the fields of:
1. education;
2. social;
3. economy; and
4. politics and culture.  c. preventing violations of women's rights in the household.

Paragraph 2 Responsibility for the Protection of Children's Rights Article 12 (1) Parents in efforts to protect Children are responsible to: a. care for, maintain, educate, and protect Children; b. develop Children according to their abilities, talents, and interests;
c. provide formal and non-formal education;
d. guide Children to behave in accordance with religion, ethics and morals as well as customs; and e. prevent marriage at the age of Children.
2. In the event that parents do not exist, or their whereabouts are unknown, or due to a reason, cannot carry out their obligations and responsibilities, then the obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1) may be transferred to the family.

Article 30

The Regency Government and the community shall organize Early Childhood Education (PAUD).

Paragraph 3 Employment Article 31 Employers are prohibited from employing Children.

Article 32 (1) The provisions as referred to in Article 31 may be exempted for Children aged between 13 (thirteen) years to 15 (fifteen) years, to perform light work as long as it does not interfere with physical, mental, and social development and health.

2. Employers who employ Children in light work as referred to in paragraph (1) must fulfill requirements including: a. written permission from parents or guardian;
b. work agreement between employer and parents or guardian; c. maximum working time of 3 (three) hours;
d. performed during the day and does not interfere with school time; e. occupational safety and health;
f. clear employment relationship; and g. receive wages in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.
3. The provisions as referred to in paragraph (2) letter a, letter b, letter f, and letter g are exempted for Children who work in their family business.

Article 33 In the event that a company employs Children as referred to in Article 32, then the workplace of Children must be separated from the workplace of adult workers.

Article 34 (1) Children may perform work in a workplace that is part of an educational or training curriculum authorized by the competent official.

2. Children as referred to in paragraph (1) must be at least 14 I (fourteen) years old.

I\ Article 35 (1) Every person is prohibited from employing and involving Children in the worst forms of work.

2. The worst forms of work as referred to in paragraph (1) include: a. all work in the form of slavery or similar practices; b. all work that exploits, provides, or offers Children for . 1. prostitution;
2. pornography production;
3. pornographic performances;
4. gambling;
5. production and trade of alcoholic beverages;
6. narcotics;
7. psychotropic substances; and
8. other addictive substances; and l or c. all work that endangers the health, safety, or morals of Children.

Paragraph 4 Social Article 36 (1) The Regency Government is obliged to provide care for neglected Children.

2. Care as referred to in paragraph (1) may be carried out by Community Organizations in the social field.
3. In the event that a Child is neglected because for some reason the parents neglect their obligations, then the Family or Regency Government or Community Organization as referred to in paragraph (2) may submit an application to the court to determine the Child as a neglected Child.
4. The court determination as referred to in paragraph (3) simultaneously determines the shelter and care for the neglected Child concerned.

Article 37 (1) The Regency Government shall organize skills training for street Children and neglected Children.

2. Skills training as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be organized by Regional Apparatus whose duties and functions are in the social and employment fields and may cooperate with Community Organizations.

Article 38 (1) The Regency Government shall provide shelters to foster street Children and social institutions for neglected Children.

2. Shelters as referred to in paragraph (1) may be organized in cooperation with Community Organizations.
3. Further provisions regarding shelters as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be regulated by Regent Regulation Article 39 The implementation of protection for Women and Children shall be carried out by Regional Apparatus whose duties and functions are related to the protection of Women and Children.

CHAPTER IV

PREVENTION

Part One

Regency Government Article 13 (1) Efforts made by the Regency Government in order to prevent violations of the rights of Women include:
a. organizing socialization regarding the prevention of violence against Women by optimizing the role of customary, cultural, religious institutions and community organizations;
b. providing guidelines on handling violence against Women;
c. involving community participation in recovery and reintegration; d. developing databases and integrated information systems on violence against Women;
e. developing family empowerment programs;
f. strengthening the function of community organizations engaged in Women's empowerment; and g. providing public facilities that are comfortable and safe for Women.
2. Efforts made by the Regency Government in order to prevent violations of the rights of Children include: a. prevention of violence against Children by optimizing the role of customary, cultural, religious institutions and community organizations; b. developing child-friendly schools;
c. encouraging increased child participation through the establishment of Children's Forums;
d. providing guidelines on handling violence against Children;
e. involving community participation in recovery and reintegration services for child victims of violence;
f. developing databases and integrated information systems on violence against Children;
g. strengthening the function of community organizations engaged in Child protection; and h. encouraging the establishment of Child-Friendly Regency.

Part Four

Law Enforcement and Legal Aid Services I, Article 49 (1) Law enforcement and legal aid services are actions of law enforcement officers provided to Women and Children victims of violence and/or witnesses.

2. Law enforcement and legal aid services as referred to in paragraph (1) are carried out by prioritizing the best interests of victims.

Article 50 (1) Law enforcement and legal aid services as referred to in article 49 are aimed at providing assistance and protection to Women and Children victims of violence and witnesses with a gender-responsive approach so that victims obtain justice and legal certainty.

2. Types of legal aid services as referred to in paragraph (1) include: a. legal aid in the reporting process and preparation of BAP; b. legal aid in the prosecution process at the prosecutor's office;
c. legal aid in the examination process at court; d. legal aid in the court hearing decision process until there is a final legal decision; and e. legal aid in the mediation process.
3. The Regency Government provides legal aid to Children and Women victims of violence free of charge.
4. In providing legal aid as referred to in paragraph (3) the Regency Government may cooperate with: a. Legal Aid Institute (LBH);
b. Women Crisis Center (WCC);
c. Advocates; and/or d. other legal assistants who are gender sensitive.

Part Three

Parents and/or Family Article 15 (1) Efforts made by the family in order to prevent violations of the rights of Women include:
a. protection of women from various violations of rights within the household; and b. encouraging the formation of harmonious, educative and democratic families;
2. Efforts made by parents in order to prevent violations of the rights of Children include: a. providing good examples to children in accordance with religious and customary teachings;
b. supervision of children in their activities inside or outside the home;
c. providing opportunities for children to engage in dialogue and express opinions democratically; and d. protection of children from various acts of violation of the rights of Children within the household.

